    <table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
    <tr>
        <td id="descriptionArea">
            <p>We plan to make prompt data products available to data right holders at data.lsst.cloud some time in 2026.  </p>

<p>In this RFC, I would like to decide what <b>butler dataset types</b> from the alert production pipelines we will publish.  Notes: </p>

<ol>
	<li>This RFC is only about the prompt image products and other types we wish to provide via a butler repo at RSP.  I don't intend to discuss catalogs provided via databases in this RFC. </li>
	<li>These datasets are produced by <tt>ap_pipe</tt> pipeline (or a fallback when full ApPipe can't run) by realtime prompt processing and catchup processing with LSSTCam data. </li>
	<li>That implies that this RFC is only about survey-style FBS-driven science observations.</li>
	<li>The data we provide to data.lsst.cloud users is a subset of what project people can access at USDF.  Project people access more types at repo <tt>embargo</tt> and  <tt>main</tt>. <b>Please think non-project users at data.lsst.cloud  in this RFC</b>! </li>
	<li>Nothing is permanent and we can change later. It might be easy to add types but difficult to remove, because users form expectations. </li>
	<li>Project requirement is to have the data products for 30 days (DMS-REQ-0310) and later on-demand re-generation (DMS-REQ-0311 , DMS-REQ-0399). We currently do not foresee a re-generation service in early 2026. </li>
	<li><del>If possible we will turn on lossy compression to save space.  But we aren’t ready to do it today. So here I start with a baseline estimate.</del>   Since 2025-12-09 prompt processing production has started to use lossy16 compression for image outputs. The size estimate here is updated with the compressed sizes.</li>
	<li>Ultimately we might consolidate some butler catalog datasets from per-
{visit, detector}
<p> into per-visit datasets in the non-realtime, post-alert, pre-publication pipelines. It might not reduce storage space but save inodes and database rows. This RFC is a baseline without such consolidation.</p></li>
	<li>Non-realtime pipelines might add dataset types for publication and they may replace some dataset types here in the future. We now have an initial Daytime AP catchup batch and its outputs are included here. Outputs from further pipelines are out of scope for this RFC but we will revisit when we know more about other non-realtime pipelines. Please consider this RFC a baseline with basic Daytime AP catchup but not other non-realtime pipelines.</li>
	<li>Some solar system dataset types (<tt>ss_*</tt> ) may be dropped if they confirm the PPDB contents are sufficient. Proposal is to provide them until PPDB is available for community. They are listed in a separate tier.</li>
</ol>




<p>This RFC may matter to you. Some examples but not limited to </p>

<ul>
	<li>Any dataset type we provide the users is a dataset type we need to provide support about.</li>
	<li>What we decide has storage implications 💰  . The data will be stored at <tt>/sdf/data/rubin/</tt>. </li>
	<li>You know how scientists may use these data</li>
	<li>… I don’t know, so here is an RFC 🙂</li>
</ul>




<p>I’m listing the dataset types in two tiers. The 3 in <font color="#0747a6">bold face</font> are images and dominate the space usage; others are much smaller by comparison.  </p>

<h2><a name="Tier1.KeepuntilDR1"></a>Tier 1.  Keep until DR1</h2>

<p>To mitigate the lack of regeneration service, we propose these 3 types to be kept as long as possible until DR1.</p>

<ul>
	<li><font color="#0747a6"><b>preliminary_visit_image</b></font> <b>(~5.3TB per night)</b></li>
	<li><tt>preliminary_visit_image_background</tt> (~0.02TB per night)</li>
	<li><tt>difference_kernel</tt> (~0.06TB per night) (Moved to Tier 2 once the regeneration service is available)</li>
	<li><tt>preliminary_visit_summary</tt>  (Produced by Daytime AP Catchup) (Typically &lt;0.01 TB per night)</li>
	<li><del>single_visit_star_reprocessed_footprints  (Produced by Daytime AP Catchup) (the 5 sigma direct source catalog) Sizes vary. Worst case can be ~5TB per night.</del>   (Replaced by <tt>ap_source</tt> )</li>
	<li><tt>ap_source</tt> (a new dataset type to be produced by Daytime AP Catchup)(the 5 sigma direct source catalog) Sizes vary and to be known.  Preliminary size estimate is  about 0.2TB per night but would be more for denser fields.</li>
</ul>




<p>If DR1 is 1 year later, it can be about 5.6TB*365 = about <b>2PB.</b> </p>

<p>If DR1 is 2 year later, it can be about 4*PB.* </p>

<p>RTN-011 v7.3 has “Start of LSST” at Feb 2026 and  DR1 at Feb 2028. </p>

<p>This assumes 1000 visits per night, but we might not have observations in full speed in early operations. Some sizes depend on the fields. </p>



<h2><a name="Tier2%3AStoredfor30days.Deleteafterwards.%C2%A0"></a>Tier 2: Stored for 30 days. Delete afterwards. </h2>

<ul>
	<li><font color="#0747a6"><b>difference_image</b></font> <b>(~5.3TB per night)</b></li>
	<li><tt>single_visit_star_footprints</tt> (typically &lt;1TB per night. Worst case can be ~5TB per night though)</li>
	<li><tt>dia_source_apdb</tt></li>
	<li><tt>dia_forced_source_apdb</tt></li>
	<li><tt>dia_object_apdb</tt></li>
	<li><tt>marginal_new_dia_source</tt></li>
	<li><tt>regionTimeInfo</tt></li>
</ul>


<p>~6TB*30= about <b>0.2PB</b> for 30 days</p>



<h2><a name="Tier3%3AKeepalluntilPPDBavailabletocommunity.Donotprovideafterwards.%C2%A0"></a>Tier 3: Keep all until PPDB available to community. Do not provide afterwards. </h2>

<ul>
	<li><tt>ss_source_direct_detector</tt></li>
	<li><tt>ss_object_direct_unassociated</tt></li>
	<li><tt>ss_object_unassociated_detector</tt></li>
	<li><tt>ss_source_detector</tt></li>
</ul>




<h2><a name="Others%C2%A0"></a>Others  </h2>

<p>Other relevant types we will also provide to data.lsst.cloud users. My understanding is they are needed per requirements (DMS-REQ-0346), so probably no disputes here. </p>

<ul>
	<li><tt>raw</tt></li>
	<li>All <tt><b>_metadata</tt>, <tt></b>_log</tt>, <tt>*_config</tt>, and packages datasets from the processing</li>
	<li><tt>refcat</tt>:  <tt>the_monster_20250219</tt></li>
	<li><tt>pretrainedModelPackage</tt>: <tt>pretrained_models/tac_cnn_dc2_2024-11-14</tt></li>
	<li><tt>skyMap</tt>: <tt>lsst_cells_v1</tt></li>
	<li><tt>template_coadd</tt>: follows what <tt>embargo</tt> has in the <tt>LSSTCam/templates</tt> collection</li>
	<li><tt>calib</tt>: follows what <tt>embargo</tt> has in the <tt>LSSTCam/calib</tt> collection</li>
</ul>


<h2><a name=""></a></h2>
<p>Types that we <b>DO NOT</b> provide to data.lsst.cloud users</p>


<ul>
	<li><font color="#0747a6"><b>template_detector</b></font> <b>(~9.4TB per night)</b>  (see comments)</li>
	<li>Any other butler dataset types from real-time/night-time prompt processing not listed in this RFC</li>
</ul>




<h2><a name="FileCounts"></a>File Counts</h2>

<p>5 Tier 1 types + 7 Tier 2 types + 4 Tier 3 types= 16 output types.</p>

<p>Plus 1 raw  + 17 tasks <b>2 ( <tt></b>_metadata</tt>, <tt>*_log</tt>)<br/>
--&gt; that is 51 types in total.  </p>

<p><tt>_config</tt> is 1 per task per day.  <tt>packages</tt> is 1 per day → not counting them</p>

<p>Types like <tt>refcat</tt> <tt>pretrainedModelPackage</tt> <tt>skyMap template_coadd</tt> and other calib should be updated less frequently than daily. → not counting them</p>



<p>51 types * 1000 visits per night * 188 detectors = about  <b>9,600,000</b> files.</p>

<p>Future work may consolidate metadata and log files so we have fewer files, but Butler database rows may still be there. </p>
            <br/>
        </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <br/>

