            [
                <font color="#336699">22/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-606818"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Tagging <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A75b6608f-df26-4775-a93d-f50ab790c78d" class="user-hover" rel="712020:75b6608f-df26-4775-a93d-f50ab790c78d" data-account-id="712020:75b6608f-df26-4775-a93d-f50ab790c78d" accountid="712020:75b6608f-df26-4775-a93d-f50ab790c78d" rel="noreferrer">Adam Bolton</a> <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=61f1af42aeaacb00720a62fb" class="user-hover" rel="61f1af42aeaacb00720a62fb" data-account-id="61f1af42aeaacb00720a62fb" accountid="61f1af42aeaacb00720a62fb" rel="noreferrer">Wei Yang</a>  as this affects storage need</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-606819"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:befe5a11-4020-4c5e-a75b-747d71151eae"
                id="word_commented_712020:befe5a11-4020-4c5e-a75b-747d71151eae"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3Abefe5a11-4020-4c5e-a75b-747d71151eae">
                    Krzysztof Findeisen
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">22/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-606819"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>My understanding (based mostly on worst-case crowded field work) is that <tt>single_visit_star_footprints</tt> can be up to 28 MiB each. That’s a scale comparable to the image datasets.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-606822"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                id="word_commented_712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6">
                    Hsin-Fang Chiang
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">22/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-606822"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Thanks! I rounded up the 30days estimate a bit. </p>

<p>But indeed if we observe crowded field every night and all are 28MiB each,  we would need  ~25% more storage… </p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-606872"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="5c2f5797a217aa69bce53f54"
                id="word_commented_5c2f5797a217aa69bce53f54"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=5c2f5797a217aa69bce53f54">
                    Jim Bosch
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">23/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-606872"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>I think we would ultimately benefit a lot from consolidating any public butler catalog datasets from per-</p>
{visit, detector}
<p> into per-visit datasets in  our post-alert, pre-publication pipelines.  I don’t think that will save regular storage space, but it may save inodes and database rows, especially if we decide to retain any butler database rows for 30-day datasets after we delete them (I think this is a big open question).</p>

<p>I think the expectation is that we will be adding a <tt>source<span class="error">&#91;_detector&#93;</span></tt> to Tier 1 in Y1 only, to be created entirely in post-alert, pre-publication pipelines.  But I think that might replace <tt>single_visit_star_detector</tt>, and it could be a lot smaller if we don’t keep the footprints and store in Parquet instead of afw.table FITS.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-606896"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09"
                id="word_commented_557058:90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09">
                    Mario Juric
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">23/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-606896"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>I want to discuss this with the SolSys subgroup, as we’re working on understanding how to increase completeness of SSO detection while we’re still bootstrapping the templates. So I don’t misrepresent them, where can I find the up-to-date definitions for <tt>single_visit_star_detector</tt> and <tt>source</tt>, and how/when they’re generated (i.e., sigma-thresholds, deblending settings, etc)?</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-606920"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab"
                id="word_commented_557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab">
                    Kian-Tat Lim
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">23/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-606920"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>We (<a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A5afef4b4-f1bc-4238-977f-9f8d3183e55c" class="user-hover" rel="557058:5afef4b4-f1bc-4238-977f-9f8d3183e55c" data-account-id="557058:5afef4b4-f1bc-4238-977f-9f8d3183e55c" accountid="557058:5afef4b4-f1bc-4238-977f-9f8d3183e55c" rel="noreferrer">Gregory Dubois-Felsmann</a> <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960" class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" data-account-id="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" accountid="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" rel="noreferrer">Tim Jenness</a> and I) believe that <tt>template_detector</tt> is trivial to reproduce using the warp task, the <tt>template_coadd</tt> that already has to be kept and published, and the WCS from the <tt>preliminary_visit_image</tt>.  So a few lines of code can replace 26 TB/night of images.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-606924"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab"
                id="word_commented_557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab">
                    Kian-Tat Lim
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">23/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-606924"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>I believe the <tt>single_visit_star_footprints</tt> contain so-called HeavyFootprints with per-pixel information (I see <tt>HeavyFootprintF</tt> in the persistables section of the FITS file).  Is this required or can it be stripped, possibly saving a lot of space?  I don’t think we’re expecting to publish HeavyFootprints as part of the standardized <tt>source</tt> Parquet catalog.</p>

<p>(Grr, Jira didn’t show me Jim’s comment when I posted this; sorry for the redundancy.)</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-607110"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438"
                id="word_commented_557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3Ab2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438">
                    Ian Sullivan
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">24/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-607110"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab" class="user-hover" rel="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab" data-account-id="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab" accountid="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab" rel="noreferrer">Kian-Tat Lim</a> <tt>template_detector</tt> is not necessarily easy to reproduce exactly. During the first ~year when we are building up incremental templates it can be very helpful to know exactly what template was used for a given science image.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-607115"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                id="word_commented_63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960">
                    Tim Jenness
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">24/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-607115"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>I had been told that templates are not changing through the first year and once we have a template for a patch that is then fixed until the templates from DR1 turn up. <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3Ab2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" class="user-hover" rel="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" data-account-id="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" accountid="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" rel="noreferrer">Ian Sullivan</a> are you saying that templates will be recalculated during the year? What is the actual reason why it’s not possible to reproduce. If you had the exact template from that date what is the problem?</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-607116"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438"
                id="word_commented_557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3Ab2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438">
                    Ian Sullivan
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">24/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-607116"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>If you have the exact <tt>template_coadd</tt> from the date it should be possible to reproduce the <tt>template_detector</tt>. There is high pressure from the community to produce alerts as early as possible over as much sky as possible, so we are preparing for the possibility that we will need to update templates several times before DR1 templates are available. Note that we only expect to have DR1 templates a year after we stop taking DR1 observations, so this period spans about two years.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-607117"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                id="word_commented_63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960">
                    Tim Jenness
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">24/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-607117"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>My naive guess is that it's still much cheaper in storage to keep every template and use butler provenance to work out which template to use to regenerate a given detector template.</p>

<p><em>This comment was left via Slack.</em></p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-607727"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                id="word_commented_63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960">
                    Tim Jenness
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">31/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-607727"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Can you add file count per day per dataset type in addition to total size? Butler is affected more by number of datasets and I see <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3Aa49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c" class="user-hover" rel="712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c" data-account-id="712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c" accountid="712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c" rel="noreferrer">David Irving</a> is currently estimating 3 million files to be published per day (more than DP1) where I think we had originally been assuming the number would be about 600k.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-607729"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                id="word_commented_63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960">
                    Tim Jenness
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">31/Oct/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-607729"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Also, if we are going to want to run a re-creation service on any of these dataset types then we will also need to be archiving the provenance graphs from prompt products. In theory we could not publish those to Google but leave them at USDF for the service to read. (we might not ever be able to promise exactly the same results unless the prompt software is very stable).</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-607903"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="5c2f5797a217aa69bce53f54"
                id="word_commented_5c2f5797a217aa69bce53f54"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=5c2f5797a217aa69bce53f54">
                    Jim Bosch
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">03/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-607903"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>I hadn’t noticed my my first quick pass through this RFC that we were proposing to store <tt>template_detector</tt>.  I think we can provide code to make them from <tt>template_coadd</tt> that will work well enough (even if it’s not quite as packaged up as a recreation service), and storing the warped templates is a huge upscope in storage.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-607911"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d"
                id="word_commented_557058:49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d">
                    Eric Bellm
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">03/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-607911"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>I don’t remember why <tt>template_detector</tt> ended up in Tier 1; I think Tier 2 (30 day retention) would be fine for it.  We should keep it that long for forced photometry and make it available for users.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-607942"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                id="word_commented_63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960">
                    Tim Jenness
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">03/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-607942"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Are all of the Tier 2 dataset types needed by the community or are some of them solely being used to make life easier for solar system processing at USDF? Keeping things for 30 days in USDF prompt butler can be done without being required to sync to cloud RSP.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608032"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                id="word_commented_712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6">
                    Hsin-Fang Chiang
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608032"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960" class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" data-account-id="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" accountid="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" rel="noreferrer">Tim Jenness</a> <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3Aa49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c" class="user-hover" rel="712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c" data-account-id="712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c" accountid="712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c" rel="noreferrer">David Irving</a>  It’s worse if we include all <tt><b>_metadata</tt> and <tt></b>_log</tt>.  Here is my math: </p>

<p>From the 2 tiers of pp products: 4+ 11 =15 output types. <br/>
Plus 1 raw  + 17 tasks <b>2 ( <tt></b>_metadata</tt>, <tt>*_log</tt>)<br/>
--&gt; that is 50 types in total.  </p>

<p><tt>_config</tt> is 1 per task per day.  <tt>packages</tt> is 1 per day → not counting them</p>

<p>Types like <tt>refcat</tt> <tt>pretrainedModelPackage</tt> <tt>skyMap template_coadd</tt> and other calib should be updated less frequently than daily. → not counting them</p>


<p>50 types * 1000 visits per night * 188 detectors =  <b>9,400,000</b> files. </p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608033"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                id="word_commented_712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6">
                    Hsin-Fang Chiang
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608033"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960" class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" data-account-id="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" accountid="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" rel="noreferrer">Tim Jenness</a>  PP currently doesn’t store any graphs.  Some work is needed to make that happen.  Can’t provenance graphs be re-created if we know the pipelines? </p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608034"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c"
                id="word_commented_712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3Aa49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c">
                    David Irving
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608034"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" data-account-id="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" accountid="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" rel="noreferrer">Hsin-Fang Chiang</a> Jim’s new provenance system consolidates all the metadata and log files into a single file.  There is still additional work needed to integrate the provenance system into PP.  Once that is done it should get things down to worst case of 1 extra dataset per exposure+detector combination – and possibly as little as one extra dataset per day_obs.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608035"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                id="word_commented_712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6">
                    Hsin-Fang Chiang
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608035"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d" class="user-hover" rel="557058:49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d" data-account-id="557058:49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d" accountid="557058:49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d" rel="noreferrer">Eric Bellm</a> : At least <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3Ab2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" class="user-hover" rel="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" data-account-id="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" accountid="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" rel="noreferrer">Ian Sullivan</a>  has expressed wishes to keep <tt>template_detector</tt> longer than 30 days if possible.  It indeed drives the storage need significantly.   <br/>
We might also pick a middle point, say 6 months or whatever, if Pipelines wish to keep <tt>template_detector</tt> longer than 30 days.  </p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608036"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                id="word_commented_712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6">
                    Hsin-Fang Chiang
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608036"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960" class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" data-account-id="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" accountid="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" rel="noreferrer">Tim Jenness</a> My understanding is that those solar system related products are indeed meant for the community, but <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09" class="user-hover" rel="557058:90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09" data-account-id="557058:90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09" accountid="557058:90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09" rel="noreferrer">Mario Juric</a>  should confirm. </p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608038"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                id="word_commented_63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960">
                    Tim Jenness
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608038"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>I don't understand. I thought above we agreed that we would not keep template_detector because it can easily be recreated so long as we track provenance for which specific template was used.</p>

<p><em>This comment was left via Slack.</em></p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608040"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09"
                id="word_commented_557058:90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A90208234-7b6a-4246-94bd-1dca1b533c09">
                    Mario Juric
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608040"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" data-account-id="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" accountid="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" rel="noreferrer">Hsin-Fang Chiang</a> <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960" class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" data-account-id="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" accountid="63e809f7db4f715c97259960" rel="noreferrer">Tim Jenness</a> Reading the Tier 2 list, these look like APDB contents (not just SolSys specific tables) + source catalogs. <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d" class="user-hover" rel="557058:49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d" data-account-id="557058:49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d" accountid="557058:49aa4cb9-303e-4a6b-9428-6c8646cbc11d" rel="noreferrer">Eric Bellm</a> should confirm, but I think these are effectively a substitute for PPDB which would mean we want the community to be able to access them.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608041"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                id="word_commented_63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960">
                    Tim Jenness
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608041"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>You mean we need them until PPDB is available? Or we need them even if PPDB exists.</p>

<p><em>This comment was left via Slack.</em></p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608059"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438"
                id="word_commented_557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3Ab2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438">
                    Ian Sullivan
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608059"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>I think there is value in keeping <tt>template_detector</tt> available, since with it and the other Tier 1 data products you can reproduce the image differencing results exactly. However, if we have provenance that will tell us the correct coadds to use as inputs and those are preserved I would support dropping the time we keep <tt>template_detector</tt> down to 30 days.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608068"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab"
                id="word_commented_557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab">
                    Kian-Tat Lim
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608068"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3Ab2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" class="user-hover" rel="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" data-account-id="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" accountid="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438" rel="noreferrer">Ian Sullivan</a> But you’re already keeping the exact difference images for 30 days.  So why do we also need <tt>template_detector</tt>?  Can’t that be reproduced exactly by subtracting the difference from the PVI?</p>

<p>(To be clear, going from 9PB to 800TB is very good.)</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608071"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438"
                id="word_commented_557058:b2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3Ab2e80694-0bc8-49fc-80b3-03ed86d92438">
                    Ian Sullivan
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608071"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab" class="user-hover" rel="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab" data-account-id="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab" accountid="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab" rel="noreferrer">Kian-Tat Lim</a> No, the image difference is the science image minus the <b>convolved</b> template, and we can’t reliably deconvolve to get the input <tt>template_detector</tt>.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608073"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab"
                id="word_commented_557058:1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A1624b468-e862-4644-aaf5-3bd8197af8ab">
                    Kian-Tat Lim
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608073"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Well, the obvious question then is can we dump <tt>difference_image</tt> if we have <tt>template_detector</tt>?  You seem to be saying that we have D = P - K * T and somehow we need to keep all four.  (Where T itself is a sum of warps of some T_c, that we’re also keeping, as I understand it.)</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-608074"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c"
                id="word_commented_712020:a49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3Aa49cc341-56b7-4dbb-8396-545e067e5c6c">
                    David Irving
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">04/Nov/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-608074"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" data-account-id="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" accountid="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" rel="noreferrer">Hsin-Fang Chiang</a> Actually it turns out that I <a href="https://rubin-obs.slack.com/archives/C07QFRSJE6R/p1762287989412839" class="external-link" rel="nofollow noreferrer">misunderstood the plan for provenance</a>.  We’ll have fewer files but the Butler database rows will still be there.  (At least for <tt>_metadata</tt>, possibly not for <tt>_logs</tt>.  And we might only have to have these internally at USDF.)  So yeah, potentially up to 9 million.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-613263"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63764f019960988ef6bcba0e"
                id="word_commented_63764f019960988ef6bcba0e"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63764f019960988ef6bcba0e">
                    Leanne Guy
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">01/Dec/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-613263"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>We need to keep  <font color="#0747a6">preliminary_visit_image </font>and <tt>preliminary_visit_image_background</tt> through DR1 – see: <a href="https://project.lsst.org/groups/ccb/node/5575" class="external-link" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><ins>https://project.lsst.org/groups/ccb/node/5575</ins></a></p>

<p>For the other Tier 1 datasets do we really need to keep them until DR1 or can we keep them until the regeneration service becomes available? Once the regeneration service becomes available, they could be moved to a Tier 2. “We currently do not foresee a re-generation service in early 2026.” → what is the ETA for the regeneration service?</p>

<p>I also understood that we were not updating templates, once produced, in year 1. <a href="http://ls.st/dmsr" class="external-link" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><ins>DMS-REQ-0280</ins></a>: “ …..but once a template is produced for a sky position and filter it will not be replaced until the next Data Release to avoid repeated baseline changes.”. This seems like a big upscope.  I understand there is pressure from the community, but year 1 alerts are best effort. </p>

<p>In general, I’d advocate for starting with the smallest possible set of data products, and add more, only if there is demand from the community during year 1. As <a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" data-account-id="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" accountid="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6" rel="noreferrer">Hsin-Fang Chiang</a> says: “ It might be easy to add types but difficult to remove, because users form expectations”</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-613279"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                id="word_commented_712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6">
                    Hsin-Fang Chiang
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">02/Dec/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-613279"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Thank you<a href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63764f019960988ef6bcba0e" class="user-hover" rel="63764f019960988ef6bcba0e" data-account-id="63764f019960988ef6bcba0e" accountid="63764f019960988ef6bcba0e" rel="noreferrer">Leanne Guy</a> . I edited the RFC for moving <tt>difference_kernel</tt>  and <tt>template_detector</tt>  to Tier 2 once the regeneration service is available. </p>


<p>I don’t know the ETA of the regeneration service. It’s not clear to me who owns that scope; but likely some watchers of this ticket know and may know the ETA? </p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-613281"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                id="word_commented_712020:34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A34995da4-22f6-4b5f-9b25-34bff6b9b0e6">
                    Hsin-Fang Chiang
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">02/Dec/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-613281"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>This RFC intentionally did not estimate the size of  <tt>template_coadd</tt> which is an input but not output of Prompt Processing. (Same for <tt>calib</tt>) . We need to make available  production <tt>template_coadd</tt>  , and mirroring what the <tt>emabrgo</tt> repo has should be good enough. (any objections?) It’s indeed an open question how long production templates need to be kept but that probably can be another RFC or a DMLT discussion. </p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-613286"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="5c2f5797a217aa69bce53f54"
                id="word_commented_5c2f5797a217aa69bce53f54"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=5c2f5797a217aa69bce53f54">
                    Jim Bosch
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">02/Dec/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-613286"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>I’m not even sold on retaining <tt>template_detector</tt> in particular (because it’s big) “until we have a regeneration service”:</p>

<ul>
	<li>We’ve already shipped DP1 without a lot of intermediates that people would similarly <em>like</em> to have, and we’re going to be doing the same for DP2.</li>
	<li>Any regeneration service that can handle the kind of heavy pixel-processing needed to regenerate these seems like it’d need to run on top of user batch, which probably means going through a TAC, and getting a SLAC account, etc.  It’s not something that would really be accessible to all science users on demand.</li>
</ul>


<p>That’s probably not consistent with requirements, which generally leaned very heavily (and with the benefit of hindsight, I think unrealistically) into regeneration to solve a lot of problems.</p>

<p>This may sound like an argument to retain those datasets longer, but I think you could make the same argument about a ton of similar DRP datasets (<tt>post_isr_image</tt>, <tt>preliminary_visit_image</tt>, <tt>direct_warp</tt>) that are also very expensive to retain, not directly expected to be useful for science, and really convenient for regenerating the direct-science data products efficiently.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-613287"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                id="word_commented_63e809f7db4f715c97259960"
                href="https://rubinobs.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=63e809f7db4f715c97259960">
                    Tim Jenness
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">02/Dec/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-613287"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>We agreed above that template detector was not needed even without a regeneration service since we can easily provide the few lines of code needed to reproduce it</p>

<p><em>This comment was left via Slack.</em></p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-613404"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="63764f019960988ef6bcba0e"
                id="word_commented_63764f019960988ef6bcba0e"
